From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 99991: let a client go when the HAL says it is gone

The agent adds clients from the BSS refresh reply and removes them only on a
disassociation event. A client that leaves without one - after a BTM steer, or
simply by walking out of range - stays on ap->stalist forever, and the agent
keeps reporting it to the controller.

Reproduced deterministically on hardware 2026-08-03 by steering a client from
one node to another, which is a much better handle on this than waiting for a
physical roam:

	steer 8g -> 4g, client obeyed and moved

	8g (old node):  HAL 0   iw 0   agent 3   <- holds a client it does not have
	4g (new node):  HAL 4   iw 1   agent 0

	controller sta_mld: own = 3a:fb:2f:94:51:7b (8g), age 5 s

The database is wrong and freshly written: the old node keeps sending a report
that looks perfectly valid, and the controller has no way to tell it is
imaginary. That is why this belongs in the agent - by the time it reaches the
controller the information is indistinguishable from truth.

bss_stations_data_cb() already fetches the current station list from the HAL
once a minute, so the authority is right there; it was only ever used to add.
This marks every known client before parsing the reply and releases the ones
the reply did not mention.

STA_ABSENT_MAX is 2 rather than 1 on purpose. Empty replies have been observed
from a healthy HAL during radio bring-up, and dropping every client on one of
those would be a worse failure than the ghost. Two consecutive misses at
BSS_REFRESH_INTERVAL means a departed client is released after about two
minutes - not instant, but bounded, which it was not before.

Removal goes through wifi_del_sta() so refcounts and notifications follow the
same path as an ordinary disassociation.

Not fixed here: the new node is slow to pick the client up (measured 2026-08-03:
HAL to agent at least 37 s, agent to controller up to 27 s). Releasing the old
entry does not make the new one arrive any sooner, so a client is briefly known
to nobody. Steering must therefore still judge freshness rather than trust
presence.
--- a/src/agent.h
+++ b/src/agent.h
@@ -264,6 +264,10 @@
 /* enum netif_type { IFTYPE_WIRED, IFTYPE_WIFI, IFTYPE_UNKNOWN }; */
 
 #define BSS_REFRESH_INTERVAL		60000       /** 1 min */
+/** Consecutive BSS refreshes a client may be missing from the HAL reply
+  * before the agent lets it go. One tolerates a lost or empty reply; the
+  * client is released after ~2 min at the refresh interval above. */
+#define STA_ABSENT_MAX			2
 
 #define NBR_REFRESH_INTERVAL		600000      /** 10 mins */
 #define NBR_AGEOUT_INTERVAL		1800000U    /** not dynamic; so 30mins is good */
@@ -419,6 +423,8 @@
 	uint8_t mld_macaddr[6];
 	uint8_t ruid[6];
 #endif
+	uint8_t absent;                 /** consecutive refreshes without this
+                                     *  client in the HAL reply */
 	uint32_t caps;                  /** capability bitmap */
 	int rssi[WIFI_NUM_ANTENNA];     /** rssi of last received pkt */
 #define MAX_RSSI_MEAS 2             /** must be >= 2: current and previous measurement alway required */
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -2879,8 +2879,21 @@
 	if (!tb[0])
 		return;
 
+	/* Mark. Everything already on the list is presumed gone until this
+	 * reply says otherwise - the HAL is the authority on who is attached,
+	 * and a client that leaves without a disassoc event (after a BTM, or
+	 * simply out of range) is otherwise never removed. */
+	{
+		struct sta *s;
+
+		list_for_each_entry(s, &ap->stalist, list)
+			if (s->absent < 255)
+				s->absent++;
+	}
+
 	blobmsg_for_each_attr(cur, tb[0], rem) {
 		uint8_t mac[6];
+		struct sta *added;
 
 		if (w->stations_mld) {
 			static const struct blobmsg_policy sp[] = {
@@ -2953,7 +2966,24 @@
 				continue;
 		}
 
-		wifi_add_sta(w->a, ap->ifname, mac);
+		added = wifi_add_sta(w->a, ap->ifname, mac);
+		if (added)
+			added->absent = 0;
+	}
+
+	/* Sweep. STA_ABSENT_MAX gives one refresh of tolerance so a lost or
+	 * momentarily empty reply does not drop live clients. */
+	{
+		struct sta *s, *tmp;
+
+		list_for_each_entry_safe(s, tmp, &ap->stalist, list) {
+			if (s->absent < STA_ABSENT_MAX)
+				continue;
+
+			dbg("%s: %s: releasing " MACFMT ", absent from %u replies\n",
+			    __func__, ap->ifname, MAC2STR(s->macaddr), s->absent);
+			wifi_del_sta(w->a, ap->ifname, s->macaddr);
+		}
 	}
 
 	w->parsed = true;
